{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EYiaGJEEjbuqWk5UugF1oe","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (64.6KB). Full output saved to: /home/testpwn/.claude/projects/-home-testpwn-huntress-2026/f1211439-e94b-4837-908f-ec92ba91c12b/tool-results/bvw3q5iy8.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Day 7 — Node by Node (script-analysis, node operation graphs)\n\nConcept: one evidence.zip with 61 members (PowerShell samples, JScript/Batch/VBScript/Python/Bash/Node operation\ngraphs); each graph decodes to a plan (steps/adapter/campaign/indicator) and is sorted into a role.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes. TT attempt 26b7da83 failed because its download was the GM body `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` (24 B saved as evidence.zip, sha != contract sha256), so every field came out None/default. The harvested variants 49b73141 + 7d6f8d53 were already answered 469/469 by the current solver (spot-checked against the raw bytes; their expected.json is solver-derived, not GM-verified). Parse rule: if every artifact is a small `{\"error\": ...}` JSON, exit non-zero (solve.py now does) instead of guessing roles. The bytes-less case was removed from cases/ (still kept under time_trial/attempts/26b7da83).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none (Day 8 TT). The start returned 503 `training_rotation_invalid` with no attempt and no artifact, so there were no variant bytes. The solver answers the TT contract's 615 ids 615/615 on the single's bytes. Rule: a start error is an orchestrator retry, not a solver change.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509). Same 5-set bundle shape (outer zip holding evidence-01..05.zip) as the GM-verified auto-mastery cases; the solver answered 3075/3075 but the attempt EXPIRED (submit RemoteDisconnected after 5.42 s solve on Homebase). expected.json = solver output, spot-checked against the raw bytes (PS base64 record, VBS Codec_ field, While/if-errorlevel control). Speed fix: sets solve in forked processes (group_by_set + ProcessPoolExecutor, sequential fallback); the nested-zip index no longer decrypts every script leaf just to sniff for PK (375 PBKDF2s -> only the members asked, per set, in parallel); powerplay.xor is one big-int XOR.\n\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1211439-e94b-4837-908f-ec92ba91c12b","uuid":"cc3c9271-d7d5-41a5-a821-edb194e0dafe","timestamp":"2026-10-29T13:04:09.735Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Day 7 — Node by Node (script-analysis, node operation graphs)\n\nConcept: one evidence.zip with 61 members (PowerShell samples, JScript/Batch/VBScript/Python/Bash/Node operation\ngraphs); each graph decodes to a plan (steps/adapter/campaign/indicator) and is sorted into a role.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes. TT attempt 26b7da83 failed because its download was the GM body `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` (24 B saved as evidence.zip, sha != contract sha256), so every field came out None/default. The harvested variants 49b73141 + 7d6f8d53 were already answered 469/469 by the current solver (spot-checked against the raw bytes; their expected.json is solver-derived, not GM-verified). Parse rule: if every artifact is a small `{\"error\": ...}` JSON, exit non-zero (solve.py now does) instead of guessing roles. The bytes-less case was removed from cases/ (still kept under time_trial/attempts/26b7da83).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none (Day 8 TT). The start returned 503 `training_rotation_invalid` with no attempt and no artifact, so there were no variant bytes. The solver answers the TT contract's 615 ids 615/615 on the single's bytes. Rule: a start error is an orchestrator retry, not a solver change.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509). Same 5-set bundle shape (outer zip holding evidence-01..05.zip) as the GM-verified auto-mastery cases; the solver answered 3075/3075 but the attempt EXPIRED (submit RemoteDisconnected after 5.42 s solve on Homebase). expected.json = solver output, spot-checked against the raw bytes (PS base64 record, VBS Codec_ field, While/if-errorlevel control). Speed fix: sets solve in forked processes (group_by_set + ProcessPoolExecutor, sequential fallback); the nested-zip index no longer decrypts every script leaf just to sniff for PK (375 PBKDF2s -> only the members asked, per set, in parallel); powerplay.xor is one big-int XOR.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D23). It is permanently UNVERIFIABLE: the D8 attempt EXPIRED (no GM grade), so expected.json stays {}. The D23 solver's 3075 answers equal the D8 solver's submission (mastery/attempts/7432c509/last_timed.json) 3075/3075, and that solver's other D8 Mastery runs were GM-verified. The real failure was SPEED: ~1100 serial AES PBKDF2s on the D23 Mastery bundle. Rule: the WinZip-AES salt sits right after each local header, so derive every member key up front in a thread pool (OpenSSL PBKDF2 releases the GIL) and look it up in the pyzipper PBKDF2 patch (solve.py `_prefetch_keys`/`_KEYS`). Profiled time dropped from 9.8 s to 5.1 s on auto-mastery-e24c070d; answers unchanged.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D24 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075, the same answers as before this change). The real failure was SPEED on the single (2.61 s under load against a 2.5 s budget). Rule: a one-set contract now splits its questions BY ARTIFACT across forked workers (k = min(#artifacts, CPUs in the affinity mask, 4); same ProcessPoolExecutor path as Mastery sets, sequential fallback, output re-sorted into contract order), and the node JScript decode runs in a thread that overlaps the Python analysis (joined only when the first JS member is analysed). Single: 1.4 s -> 0.6 s (12 CPU), 1.0 s with `taskset -c 0,1`; answers identical to the sequential run (1516/1516 GM-verified).\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D8 Mastery 7432c509, re-flagged on D25 -- still UNVERIFIABLE, expected.json {}; solver fills 3075/3075 in 0.66 s, single 1571/1571 GM-verified in 0.38 s, 0.4-0.5 s pinned to 2 CPUs). The flagged 2.59 s single / in-window timeout was measured under host load (loadavg ~9 with parallel generalize workers): sequential cProfile is 0.73 s, of which ~0.2 s is 341 module-level `re.compile`s in `lib.codec` + 88 in `lib.warmups_codecs` and ~0.25 s `lib.codec.peel_plan` for the bash members (already the fast path vs the ~60 ms/file interpreter). No solver change; the remaining wins are lib-side (below).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D26 speed re-flag: single 5.61 s under host load, 1.2 s idle). Hot path was `_codec_hint` -> `lib.codec.find_codecs` (~27 ms/file, peel_plan + `_cs_fold_codecs` fallbacks) on every bash/VBS member. Rule: decode the plain `Codec_<id> = \"rep|transform|key\"` + `Material_<id>` pair directly first (`codec._CODEC_RE` + `material_text` + `decode`, skipped for `#@~^` encoded blocks); the full find_codecs scan runs only when that finds no plan. Hint is identical to find_codecs on every bash/VBS member across all cases; single 1.2 s -> 0.7 s, answers unchanged.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- `lib.codec` / `lib.warmups_codecs` (D25 re-measure): 341 + 88 regexes compiled at import = ~0.2 s of a 0.73 s sequential single. Compile lazily (e.g. a `functools.cache`d `_re(name)` accessor) -- it is now the biggest fixed cost of every script-lane solver.\n- `lib.codec` import is ~0.3 s, almost all of it from ~340 module-level `re.compile`s (cProfile, D24 single). Compiling lazily would save that on every script-lane solver start.\n- Solver skeleton: when `group_by_set` gives one group, split the questions by artifact across forked workers (independent members), like D24 solve.py `main()`.\n- `lib.common.gm_error_body(artdir) -> bytes|None`: detect a GM error JSON (`rate_limited`, ...) saved in place of an\n  artifact, so every solver fails loudly. Better still: the harness download step (hctf/hb_timed.py) should retry on\n  `{\"error\":\"rate_limited\"}` and never save it as the artifact. That would have saved this timed attempt.\n- `lib.common.iter_entries(root, leaf_ext=...)`: don't load a member just to sniff for a nested zip when its name has a script/text extension (.js/.ps1/.py/.vbs/.cmd/.bat/.sh/.mjs/.php/...). Today it decrypts every AES member of a Mastery bundle up front (375 PBKDF2 = ~0.2 s locally, many times that on Homebase), and each forked set repeats it. See `_iter_entries` in this solve.py.\n- `lib.common._zip_open`: prefetch WinZip-AES keys for all members in parallel (salt = bytes after the local header; `hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac` in a ThreadPoolExecutor), as D23 solve.py `_prefetch_keys` does. Every script-lane Mastery solver pays ~1 ms x members serially today.\n- `lib.codec`: ~295 module-level `re.compile`s cost ~0.3 s per solver start, and `_find_codecs_uncached` runs ~16 regex probes per text (4669 searches / 287 texts = 0.4 s on the D23 Mastery). Compile lazily and gate each detector on a cheap substring test (`'Codec_' in text`, ...).\n- `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` + `material_text` + `decode` reading first and return early on a plan, before the carrier/C#-fold/peel fallbacks (each script-lane solver pays ~27 ms per member for them otherwise). Also: `peel_plan`'s wall-clock `budget` makes results depend on host load.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D27 speed cycle): the single took 2-5.8 s because `analyse_node` (.mjs/.cjs members, 8 per single) ran the full `codec.find_codecs` peel scan (~170 ms/file, `_awksed_codecs`/`peel_plan`). Parse rule: decode the declared `Codec_<id>` + `Material_<id>` pair directly first (same fast path as `_codec_hint`) and fall back to `find_codecs` only when that yields no `steps=` plan -> single 0.7 s, all answers unchanged (52/52 cases pass; 7432c509 still unverifiable, 3075/3075 filled).\n\n## Proposed lib additions (D27 speed)\n- `lib.codec.find_codecs`: try the direct `_CODEC_RE` spec + `material_text` + `decode` path before `_awksed_codecs` (the peel scan dominates script-lane solver time whenever a solver calls find_codecs unconditionally).\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none in the bytes (D28 speed re-flag: \"7.99 s single\" measured under host load, loadavg 8.5-10 with 3 parallel generalize/duel workers). Direct `solve.py` on the single = 0.6-0.9 s wall (1703/1703), cProfile 1.16 s (0.33 s imports, mostly `lib.codec` regex compiles). `hctf solver-run` took 47 s, but 76 s of a timed probe was `hctf.solvers.dispatch` (re-reads all 52 case contracts + `question_index()` + difflib per question), NOT the solver: `run_solver` on the full contract = 0.69 s. No solver change; 51/52 cases pass, 7432c509 is still unverifiable (expected.json {}), 3075/3075 filled.\n\n## Proposed lib additions (D28 speed)\n- `hctf.solvers.dispatch`: cache the per-solver `own` question set (json-load of every `cases/*/contract.json`, 1-20 MB each x 52) keyed by mtime, and skip `difflib.get_close_matches` when `default_solver` already owns the question. On a loaded host this costs 45-75 s per `solver-run`, far more than the 0.7 s solve.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none (D28 generalize, compound-d27-single_solve): not a solver bug -- the case's artifacts/evidence.zip was day26's archive (sha256 f2c85777..., hardlinked from day26/day27 case dirs at 2026-10-28 07:33), not the contract's 58ac2b36.... On the real bytes (day27/scripting-from-whence-it-came/single_solve/artifacts/latest/evidence.zip) the unchanged solver answers 1659/1659 GM-verified fields; replaced the case artifact with those bytes (cp --remove-destination, link broken). Parse rule unchanged.\n\n## Proposed lib additions (D28 harness)\n- Case-builder integrity: `hctf solver-test` (or the case writer) should assert sha256(artifacts/*) == contract `sha256`/`artifacts[].sha256` and report \"case artifact mismatch\" instead of a 452-field diff. `day27/scripting-from-whence-it-came/solver/cases/single_solve/artifacts/evidence.zip` still holds day26's bytes (f2c85777...) -- outside this worker's folder, not touched; restore from that day's single_solve/artifacts/latest/.\n\nVARIANT AXIS: none -- no solver bug (D28 compound-d27 re-flag). `cases/compound-d27-single_solve/artifacts/evidence.zip` was a HARDLINK whose inode got overwritten on 2026-10-28 07:33 with another zip (sha f2c85777..., != contract sha256 58ac2b36...), so all 452 \"wrong\" fields were answers for the wrong bytes. solve.py is byte-identical to the D27 solver. Fix: broke the link and restored the original D27 download (day27/.../single_solve/artifacts/20261027T130010Z/evidence.zip) -> 1659/1659 pass. Rule: before chasing a compound-* regression, check `sha256sum artifacts/*` against contract `sha256`/`artifacts[].sha256`.\n\n## Proposed lib additions\n- hctf solver-test / case harvesting: copy (never hardlink) case artifacts, and flag any case whose artifact sha256 != contract sha256 as CORRUPT instead of reporting a solver diff. NOTE: day27/scripting-from-whence-it-came/solver/cases/single_solve/artifacts/evidence.zip is still the corrupted f2c857... bytes (outside this worker's folder) -- restore it from day27/.../single_solve/artifacts/20261027T130010Z/evidence.zip.\n{\n  \"base\": \"script-analysis-day-29-makefile-variant-diff\",\n  \"lane\": \"script-analysis\",\n  \"name\": \"📜 Make It Make Sense\",\n  \"ids\": {\n    \"single_solve\": \"script-analysis-day-29-makefile-variant-diff\",\n    \"time_trial\": \"script-analysis-day-29-makefile-variant-diff/time_trial\",\n    \"mastery\": \"script-analysis-day-29-makefile-variant-diff/mastery\"\n  }\n}{\n  \"attempt_id\": \"4c3127e8-775c-4bfb-b645-989e9ee6518a\",\n  \"mode\": \"single_solve\",\n  \"started_at\": \"2026-10-29T13:00:09.768Z\",\n  \"name\": \"📜 Make It Make Sense\",\n  \"prompt\": \"Unseen Makefile variants and their prior-language build chains differ in layered variables and target flow. Diff them carefully, and keep only the tradecraft conclusions that hold up across every variant.\",\n  \"questions